Generation and display of RUN COMMAND files:                                               (C) TANDATA   1984
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Notes on attributes - for full details see "PRESTEL Terminal specification"    
                                                              
                                                                               
When a file is READ or WRITTEN, a header is included which assists the user to 
identify the file in the directory displays. The RUN COMMAND file does no      (British Telecom)             
All attributes revert to their default settings at the start of each line.     
The default settings are:                                                      
   Alphanumeric mode, white on black background.        t have
this header, and therefore a special file extension is used to inform both the 
editor and the run command system that the file is of the correct type.        
                                                                               
This                        
   Normal height, not flashing or concealed.                                   
   Mosaic graphics are initially contiguous and not held.                      
                                                                     extension is .PRC, and although the run command system will accept files  
with different headers, the editor will only recognise .PRC as a valid file    
type without a header. When such a file is READ, the editor will prompt the    
user to warn that the run command mode is active. In this mode, the CTRL-P     
screen switch and commands HDR, NEWHDR and SEE are inactive, as the file may   
only contain normal ASCII characters, and therefore the attribute editor is    
not required. As a safeguard aghe next "@" character in the   
text. The cursor will be left at the postion after the "@". If no "@" found,   
the cursor does not move. This command is used for rapid generation of FORM    
fields (see section 7 of the User Guide).                    ainst reading a .PGE file and writing in .PRC   
mode all attributes are stripped from the screen buffer before the write takes 
place.                                                                         
